 \nTransition  | 101\n | \nwith Little Smoothness  | 133\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRiemann’s Blueprints for Architecture in Myriad Dimensions (https://ideas.repec.org/h/spr/sprchp/978-3-642-18245-7_4.html)\nciteturn563search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.7 years ago; Crawled: last month;   * Marcel Berger ... \"Riemann’s Blueprints for Architecture in Myriad Dimensions,\" Springer Books, in: A Panoramic View of Riemannian Geometry, chapter 4, pages 143-218, Springer.\n\n# Riemann’s Blueprints for Architecture in Myriad Dimensions\n\n## In: A Panoramic View of Riemannian Geometry\n\n## Author\n\nListed:\n\n  * Marcel Berger\n\n(Institut des Hautes Études Scientifiques IHES)\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nAs we said in chapter 2, Riemann’s construction of the Riemannian manifold consisted first in building the foundation of the smooth manifold. He then established on that foundation the concept of a Riemannian metric. In the first two sections we will present smooth manifolds, and thereafter define Riemannian metrics. The notion of smooth manifold is at the same time extremely natural and quite hard to define correctly. This notion started with Riemann in 1854 and was widely used. Hermann Weyl was the first to lay down solid foundations for this notion in 1923. The definition became completely clear in the famous article Whitney 1936 [1259].\n\n## Suggested Citation\n\n* Marcel Berger, 2003. \"Riemann’s Blueprints for Architecture in Myriad Dimensions,\" Springer Books, in: A Panoramic View of Riemannian Geometry, chapter 4, pages 143-218, Springer.\nHandle: RePEc:spr:sprchp:978-3-642-18245-7_4\nDOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-18245-7_4\n\n## More about this item\n\n### Keywords\n\n[Input][Button: Riemannian Manifold]\n\n;\n\n[Input][Button: Tangent Space]\n\n;\n\n[Input][Button: Symmetric Space]\n\n;\n\n[Input][Button: Sectional Curvature]\n\n;\n\n[Input][Button: Curvature Tensor]\n\n;\n\n[Input][Button: All these keywords]\n--